WEBVTT

1
00:00:10.843 --> 00:00:13.844
life for the March girls began bright and early the next day.

2
00:00:15.365 --> 00:00:18.766
Unfortunately, neither Meg nor Jo was in the best of moods.

3
00:00:19.886 --> 00:00:25.188
Like most siblings, they did what they always do when things are annoying and irritable: they took it

4
00:00:25.308 --> 00:00:26.088
out on each other.

5
00:00:27.272 --> 00:00:31.235
"Hurry up, Jo," snapped Meg as she put on her thick winter coat.

6
00:00:31.255 --> 00:00:36.279
"Why? Are you so anxious to see your precious King children?"

7
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:38.101
Jo retorted nastily.

8
00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:41.443
"Must you doodle at your desk every morning?

9
00:00:42.164 --> 00:00:44.346
For once can't you keep an eye on the clock?"

10
00:00:44.646 --> 00:00:45.587
Meg spat back.

11
00:00:46.527 --> 00:00:50.651
Jo woke up at the crack of dawn every morning and rushed up to her sanctuary where she

12
00:00:50.671 --> 00:00:52.212
"doodled" on her latest story.

13
00:00:53.364 --> 00:00:58.426
She called it her "pre-day exercise" and often forgot the time until one of the others had to

14
00:00:58.466 --> 00:00:59.427
go up and remind her.

15
00:00:59.447 --> 00:01:04.569
"Girls, do you think it's wise to start the New Year off like this?"

16
00:01:05.269 --> 00:01:07.510
Mother gave the girls a quick kiss and hug.

17
00:01:08.771 --> 00:01:14.013
As she did every cold winter day, she had baked two buns for them, not for eating but

18
00:01:14.073 --> 00:01:16.534
to keep their hands warm from the frigid temperature.

19
00:01:17.934 --> 00:01:21.196
She slipped one inside each of the girls' coat pockets.

20
00:01:22.204 --> 00:01:26.146
Immediately the girls felt a surge of warmth spread across their stomachs.

21
00:01:27.186 --> 00:01:32.168
Perhaps it was that or Mother's gentle smile that made Meg and Jo stop arguing.

22
00:01:32.188 --> 00:01:35.690
"We'll try harder, Mother," promised Meg.

23
00:01:35.710 --> 00:01:40.232
"It's my fault and I apologize, Meg," admitted Jo.

24
00:01:42.633 --> 00:01:45.114
The sisters started out on the path toward the street.

25
00:01:46.595 --> 00:01:51.537
As part of their routine, they looked back before turning the corner, for Mother was always at the

26
00:01:51.577 --> 00:01:53.458
window, smiling and waving at them.

27
00:01:54.999 --> 00:02:00.262
No matter what happened or whatever mood they were in, that small gesture filled their hearts with love

28
00:02:00.462 --> 00:02:00.822
and hope.

29
00:02:02.763 --> 00:02:06.865
Today Jo caught a glimpse of someone else looking out a window next door.

30
00:02:07.766 --> 00:02:12.848
It was Laurie, looking pathetically tiny in comparison with the massive house he lived in.

31
00:02:12.868 --> 00:02:14.589
"There's Laurie!"

32
00:02:14.850 --> 00:02:16.050
Jo cried excitedly.

33
00:02:16.891 --> 00:02:19.132
She held up her foot and wiggled it at him.

34
00:02:20.224 --> 00:02:21.764
He promptly returned the greeting.

35
00:02:22.745 --> 00:02:27.006
Then to Jo's astonishment, Meg also lifted her foot and waved it.

36
00:02:27.026 --> 00:02:30.927
"Meg!" cried her astonished sister.

37
00:02:30.947 --> 00:02:37.049
"I'm only showing him that my ankle is better now, thanks to him," was Meg's explanation.

38
00:02:38.409 --> 00:02:42.490
Giggling and linking their arms to stay warm, the sisters hurried along the street.

39
00:02:44.371 --> 00:02:49.860
After a particularly trying day at Aunt March's, Jo dragged her feet home, eager to curl up on

40
00:02:49.900 --> 00:02:51.061
the sofa and read her book.

41
00:02:53.042 --> 00:02:57.624
As she approached the familiar grounds, something made her lift her head up in the direction of the

42
00:02:57.684 --> 00:02:58.624
Laurences' mansion.

43
00:02:59.685 --> 00:03:04.387
In the same window he had appeared in that morning, there stood Laurie, wearing what seemed to be

44
00:03:04.427 --> 00:03:05.767
the same attire as well.

45
00:03:05.787 --> 00:03:10.649
"That lazy boy hasn't changed all day," Jo remarked.

46
00:03:10.669 --> 00:03:12.850
"Hello, Jo!"

47
00:03:13.170 --> 00:03:16.532
Laurie opened his window and shouted down at her in a hoarse voice.

48
00:03:18.432 --> 00:03:19.313
"Are you sick?"

49
00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:20.514
Jo yelled back.

50
00:03:21.655 --> 00:03:25.459
The sudden cold air from the open window set Laurie off in a coughing fit.

51
00:03:25.479 --> 00:03:30.364
"Don't bother answering that question," Jo replied firmly.

52
00:03:30.384 --> 00:03:32.606
"I can see you're not well.

53
00:03:32.866 --> 00:03:33.567
Just stay put.

54
00:03:33.807 --> 00:03:34.648
I'm coming over!"

55
00:03:35.569 --> 00:03:37.651
If anything, Jo was loyal to her friends.

56
00:03:37.951 --> 00:03:42.556
If they were sick in bed, she would make any sacrifices necessary to cheer them up.

57
00:03:44.204 --> 00:03:49.047
Forgetting her own fatigue, Jo sprinted inside her house to change and gather a few things for her

58
00:03:49.107 --> 00:03:49.687
sick neighbor.

59
00:03:51.068 --> 00:03:53.509
Of course Jo had to get Mother's permission first.

60
00:03:54.349 --> 00:03:59.472
When Mother heard how sick Laurie was, she got Jo to take over some homemade soup.

61
00:04:00.593 --> 00:04:06.336
Jo did not often lack words to express anything but when she stepped into the Laurence mansion a

62
00:04:06.376 --> 00:04:08.697
few minutes later, she was speechless.

63
00:04:11.078 --> 00:04:17.501
Dark, exquisite furniture decorated the foyer, each piece intricately carved in exotic Asian wood.

64
00:04:18.702 --> 00:04:22.083
A massive crystal chandelier hung stately in the center of the hall.

65
00:04:23.504 --> 00:04:26.485
Large oil paintings of the Laurence men hung on the walls.

66
00:04:27.606 --> 00:04:31.467
There was one of a younger man who had the same mischievous grin as Laurie.

67
00:04:32.488 --> 00:04:35.829
Jo concluded that he must be Laurie's deceased father.

68
00:04:36.729 --> 00:04:39.951
She recognized the painting right next to that as old Mr.

69
00:04:39.971 --> 00:04:42.652
Laurence. "Does he frighten you?"

70
00:04:43.088 --> 00:04:45.390
a scratchy voice called from the top of the stairs.

71
00:04:46.571 --> 00:04:51.274
Jo looked up to see Laurie, in a maroon silk dressing gown, gazing down at her with a

72
00:04:51.354 --> 00:04:52.114
teasing grin.

73
00:04:52.134 --> 00:04:58.078
"I don't get frightened of anything or anyone," Jo stated overconfidently.

74
00:04:59.159 --> 00:05:03.222
Inside, however, she admitted to herself that if she came across Mr.

75
00:05:03.242 --> 00:05:06.624
Laurence face-to-face, she might feel a little bit intimidated.

76
00:05:06.644 --> 00:05:11.688
"Don't just stand there staring," Laurie's cheerful voice broke into her thoughts.

77
00:05:12.680 --> 00:05:14.461
"What did you bring for the sick boy?"

78
00:05:15.581 --> 00:05:18.202
Jo had almost forgotten about the basket of goodies.

79
00:05:18.222 --> 00:05:20.942
"Mother made a fresh pot of soup.

80
00:05:21.482 --> 00:05:24.443
When I told her about you, she sent it over immediately."

81
00:05:25.504 --> 00:05:29.465
Lowering his eyes, Laurie said quietly, "I wish she was my mother.

82
00:05:30.425 --> 00:05:32.926
In fact, I have a secret to tell you."

83
00:05:34.006 --> 00:05:36.707
Jo, who loved secrets, urged him to tell her.

84
00:05:36.727 --> 00:05:41.748
"But you have to promise you won't laugh," he said solemnly.

85
00:05:42.876 --> 00:05:49.660
"Okay," Jo said with uncertainty, for she found it very hard not to laugh when she thought something

86
00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:50.760
was truly funny.

87
00:05:51.921 --> 00:05:54.603
Laurie came down the stairs and stood next to Jo.

88
00:05:55.543 --> 00:05:58.725
Jo did not realize how tall he was until that moment.

89
00:05:59.805 --> 00:06:02.167
"I have often watched you and your sister play.

90
00:06:03.227 --> 00:06:07.470
From the window in my room, I can look into your living room," he said shyly.

91
00:06:07.490 --> 00:06:10.311
"Oh, you naughty boy!

92
00:06:10.451 --> 00:06:11.712
You were spying on us!"

93
00:06:12.124 --> 00:06:13.045
Jo exclaimed.

94
00:06:13.065 --> 00:06:14.907
"No, no!"

95
00:06:15.247 --> 00:06:16.108
protested Laurie.

96
00:06:16.908 --> 00:06:17.929
"I wasn't spying.

97
00:06:18.510 --> 00:06:25.856
I was merely curious and," he paused, "a little jealous at how happy your family always seems to

98
00:06:25.896 --> 00:06:31.480
be." Jo and her family knew of Laurie's misfortunes, though not in detail.

99
00:06:32.982 --> 00:06:36.505
His parents had died in a tragic accident when he was a small boy.

100
00:06:37.506 --> 00:06:42.982
Having no other relations except for his grandfather, Laurie had been sent to boarding schools abroad.

101
00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:47.445
Then last year, his grandfather had brought Laurie over to live with him.

102
00:06:47.465 --> 00:06:51.828
"I've always wanted a big family," he said wistfully.

103
00:06:52.969 --> 00:06:57.812
Something tugged at Jo's heart and she longed to give him a big hug just as she would

104
00:06:57.852 --> 00:06:58.833
have with her sisters.

105
00:06:58.853 --> 00:07:04.777
"Well, you have my permission to watch us whenever you like," she replied.

106
00:07:04.797 --> 00:07:10.678
"In fact, I need to ask Mother first, but I'm sure she will be more than happy to

107
00:07:10.738 --> 00:07:11.459
have you visit."

108
00:07:11.479 --> 00:07:14.261
"Thank you, Jo," he said sincerely.

109
00:07:14.281 --> 00:07:16.103
"I would really like that.

110
00:07:16.903 --> 00:07:21.107
Now, perhaps I should test that soup out to see whether it has gotten cold or not."

111
00:07:23.009 --> 00:07:26.292
So the two friends walked to the kitchen, laughing all the way.

112
00:07:27.653 --> 00:07:31.857
Laurie and Jo chatted away like two old friends with so much in common.

113
00:07:32.817 --> 00:07:34.619
Jo told him all about her sisters.

114
00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:41.843
"Well, now I'm happy to know who Beth is and who Amy is," said Laurie, who had been

115
00:07:41.883 --> 00:07:44.064
confused about the younger girls' names.

116
00:07:44.084 --> 00:07:50.068
"By the way, Beth sent this over to you," Jo said, taking out an old rag doll that

117
00:07:50.108 --> 00:07:50.888
Beth treasured.

118
00:07:50.908 --> 00:07:57.812
"Whenever she feels sick, Lucy," Jo pointed, indicating the doll's name, "makes her feel better."

119
00:07:59.404 --> 00:08:05.007
Slapping his knee, Laurie nearly tumbled off his chair, but stopped suddenly when he caught sight of someone

120
00:08:05.087 --> 00:08:06.148
standing in the doorway.

121
00:08:06.168 --> 00:08:13.651
"Why aren't you in bed resting, Theodore, instead of acting like a silly monkey?"

122
00:08:14.412 --> 00:08:17.553
the old man said in a dangerously low voice.

123
00:08:19.034 --> 00:08:19.594
Old Mr.

124
00:08:19.614 --> 00:08:22.936
Laurence was home and he was not happy.
